          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pōtō, pōtāre — \"to drink, to drink deep\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Pōtōrēs usque ad mediam noctem pōtant. The drinkers drink on until midnight."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pōtō, pōtāre, pōtāvī, pōtātum or pōtum. Stronger than bibō: drinking hard, drinking for pleasure. English potion and potable come from the same root."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pōtō is inherited from PIE peh₃- 'to drink', with Greek pinō 'to drink' as a cousin (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English potion and potable come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
